n that includes our current recommendation (vote) and evidence.\n\nWe have our own notes. We can decide to either support B or propose another candidate. Let's evaluate.\n\nCandidate A: quick correct decisions, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education, known 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere with crew. Strengths: reliability, decision speed, crew atmosphere. Weaknesses: communication difficulty, egocentric, unwillingness to learn.\n\nCandidate C: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. Strengths: situational awareness, technical competence, leadership. Weaknesses: feedback orientation, organization.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as arrogant, relatively weak leadership skills. Strengths: problem solving, responsibility, concentration. Weaknesses: arrogance, weak leadership, possibly crew cooperation.\n\nWe need to bring up at least one candidate not yet discussed. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate A's reliability and crew atmosphere are strong, and his communication difficulty may be less severe than B's uncooperative behavior. Or we could highlight C's anticipation of dangerous situations as crucial for safety.\n\nGiven the panel hasn't discussed A, C, D, we should bring them in. We can perhaps argue that Candidate A's 100% reliability and positive crew atmosphere outweigh his communication difficulty, making him a strong contender. Or we could argue that Candidate C's ability to anticipate dangerous situations is critical for long-distance flights, and his leadership is strong, though he struggles with criticism.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. We could either stay neutral, but we must state a recommendation. We could say we still support B but want to discuss A's reliability and crew atmosphere. Or we could shift to A.\n\nLet's weigh: B has strong reliability and stress handling, but uncooperative and grumpy. A is 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere, but difficulty communicating ideas and egocentric. Which is more concerning? Communication difficulty could affect crew cooperation. Egocentric may also affect teamwork. B's uncooperative behavior also affects crew. Both have issues.\n\nC has strong situational awareness, leadership, but unorganized and not good at criticism. That could affect safety.\n\nD has responsibility, problem solving, but arrogance and weak leadership.\n\nGiven the criteria, reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nB: reliability (very conscientious), stress resilience (handles stress well), technical competence (weather, computer), but communication issues (pretentious, uncooperative). No info on decision quality, attention, adaptability.\n\nA: reliability (known 100% reliable), decision quality (quick correct decisions), crew atmosphere positive, but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). No info on stress resilience, technical competence.\n\nC: anticipates dangerous situations (decision quality, stress?), sees complex connections (technical competence), leadership, but unorganized, not good at criticism (adaptability, crew cooperation). No info on reliability.\n\nD: responsibility, problem solving, concentration, responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience?), but arrogance, weak leadership, maybe crew cooperation issues.\n\nGiven the missing info, B still seems strong on core reliability and stress. But we need to discuss others.\n\nI think we can keep B as provisional but raise concerns and ask for more info on A's stress handling and technical competence, and C's reliability, etc. But we need to give a vote. We could vote for B